    <h1 class="rv in" style="font-size:clamp(2.2rem,5vw,4rem);max-width:20ch">Can You Swim at the Foot of Cabo Girão?</h1>
    <div class="artmeta rv in d1"><span>20 November 2026</span><span>6 min read</span><span>Funchal · Madeira</span></div>
  </div>
</header>

<section class="pad">
  <div class="wrap">
    <article class="article rv">
      <p class="lede">You can, in the sense that nothing physically stops a strong swimmer, but from a boat it is a poor choice. The foot of Cabo Girão is a boulder beach under a 580 metre cliff that still sheds rock, so boats keep their distance and swim a few minutes further west, at Fajã dos Padres.</p>

      <h2>Why don't boats stop for a swim right under the cliff?</h2><p>Because the cliff is not finished. The flat ground and the beach at the base of Cabo Girão exist because the wall above has collapsed, again and again. According to the regional geology survey, the ground down there is landslide debris: one large fall came in 1930, and loose blocks still come down every so often. Most of the time nothing happens. The problem is that you never know which afternoon is the exception.</p><p>A rock does not have to be big to hurt someone after a fall of several hundred metres. And unlike on a beach path, a swimmer in the water cannot step aside.</p><h2>Is rockfall really a risk on this coast?</h2><p>Yes, all along Madeira's south coast, not only at Cabo Girão. A few recent examples:</p><ul><li>In September 2021 part of the slope above Praia Formosa in Funchal collapsed onto the promenade, and two people were taken to hospital as a precaution.</li><li>In June 2022 large stones fell onto the seafront road in Calheta, and part of the road was closed while the slope was checked.</li><li>Over one night in February 1992, part of Penha d'Águia on the north coast slid into the sea, and the new land it made stretched almost 200 metres.</li></ul><p>Heavy rain and strong swell both loosen rock. None of this is a reason to fear the coast. It is a reason to choose where you float.</p><h2>What else makes the foot of Cabo Girão a bad swim spot?</h2><p>Rockfall is the main reason, but not the only one. A good skipper runs through the rest without thinking about it:</p><ol><li><strong>The shore is boulders.</strong> There is no sandy shelf to stand on. Any swell turns the waterline into a place where waves push you onto rock and pull back hard.</li><li><strong>The water gets deep fast.</strong> Off this coast the edge of the island's underwater shelf lies very close to land. That is great for colour, less great for holding an anchor near a cliff.</li><li><strong>A boat must be able to leave at once.</strong> If something starts to fall or the swell picks up, the skipper wants to be moving in seconds, with everyone back on board. That is hard to do with guests in the water right under the wall.</li><li><strong>It is a protected area.</strong> The region gave the cliff and its stretch of sea protected status in 2017. It is a place to look at with care, not to crowd.</li><li><strong>The view is better from further out.</strong> Under the cliff you see a wall. A few hundred metres off, you see the whole thing, top to bottom.</li></ol><h2>Where is the swim stop instead?</h2><p>A few minutes west, at Fajã dos Padres. It is about a nautical mile past the cliff, still under high ground, but the bay is wider, the shore is lived on and farmed, and the boat can sit well off the base of the rock in calmer water. The sea there is deep and blue, and on a calm day you can see far down from the deck.</p><p>On the Chifbay <a href="/hidden-coves-half-day">Day Trip</a>, the order is simple. You look up at Cabo Girão from the water while the photos are taken, with the drone filming overhead if the weather is good enough. The swimming waits for Fajã dos Padres, a few minutes on: jump off the boat, swim, or try a paddle board there. Ribeira Brava is the second place to get in, and then the boat heads quickly home. Read more about that stop on the <a href="/faja-dos-padres-boat-trip">Fajã dos Padres boat trip</a> page.</p><p>The Sunset Trip is different: it has no swimming at all, so the question does not come up.</p><h2>Who decides where you swim?</h2><p>The skipper, every time. They look at the swell direction and height, the wind, the state of the shore, other boats and how confident the group is in the water. If Fajã dos Padres is not comfortable on the day, they choose another spot, or say plainly that today is a look but not a swim day. On a day too rough for any trip, Chifbay's boat stays in the marina: you move to another date or get all your money back. The skipper decides that too. We explain the thinking in detail in <a href="/posts/how-the-skipper-chooses-the-swim-stop">how a skipper chooses where to stop for a swim</a>.</p><h2>Can you swim there if you go down by cable car?</h2><p>That is a different situation. The Rancho cable car takes visitors down to the fajãs at the foot of Cabo Girão, and there is a small beach at the bottom. If you swim there, you are on your own judgement, under the same cliff, so read any signs at the station, stay away from the base of the rock, and leave the water if the sea is moving. Fajã dos Padres has its own cable car from Quinta Grande if you would rather swim there from land.</p><h2>Who should not swim on a boat trip at all?</h2><p>That is about you more than about the place. Open water off Madeira is deep from the first stroke, and there is no bottom to stand on. If you are not a confident swimmer, stay on board or stay close to the boat and the ladder, and tell the skipper before anyone jumps. If you want a buoyancy aid or have a medical condition that matters in the water, ask us on WhatsApp before booking.</p><p>Jumping in is only for the spot the skipper points to, feet first, after they have checked the depth and the area around the boat. Diving head first is a bad idea anywhere on this coast. If you want the bigger picture on cliff jumps, our <a href="/posts/cliff-jumping-in-madeira-guide">guide to cliff jumping in Madeira</a> covers the places where it is done and the places where it should not be.</p><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>Is swimming banned at Cabo Girão?</h3><p>We have not found a general ban. The base is a protected area with active rockfall, so boats keep away and swim at Fajã dos Padres instead.</p><h3>How far is Fajã dos Padres from Cabo Girão by boat?</h3><p>About one nautical mile west, a few minutes at cruising speed.</p><h3>Can you swim on the Sunset Trip?</h3><p>No. The Sunset Trip has no swim stop, and the paddle boards only come out on the Day Trip.</p><h3>Is the water deep at the swim stop?</h3><p>Yes. The sea floor drops quickly off Madeira, so you cannot stand. Stay close to the boat if you are not a strong swimmer.</p>

      <blockquote>Look up at Cabo Girão from the boat, then swim where the cliff has already done its falling.</blockquote>
    </article>
    <p class="pref-src" data-pref-source><span>Enjoying our Madeira guides?</span> <a href="https://www.google.com/preferences/source?q=chifbay.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="#4285F4" d="M23.5 12.3c0-.8-.1-1.6-.2-2.3H12v4.5h6.5a5.6 5.6 0 0 1-2.4 3.6v3h3.9c2.2-2.1 3.5-5.1 3.5-8.8z"/><path fill="#34A853" d="M12 24c3.2 0 6-1.1 7.9-2.9l-3.9-3c-1.1.7-2.4 1.2-4 1.2-3.1 0-5.7-2.1-6.6-4.9h-4v3.1A12 12 0 0 0 12 24z"/><path fill="#FBBC05" d="M5.4 14.4a7.2 7.2 0 0 1 0-4.7V6.6h-4a12 12 0 0 0 0 10.8l4-3z"/><path fill="#EA4335" d="M12 4.8c1.7 0 3.3.6 4.6 1.8l3.4-3.4A12 12 0 0 0 1.4 6.6l4 3.1C6.3 6.9 8.9 4.8 12 4.8z"/></svg>Add Chifbay as a preferred source on Google</a></p>

